Found by mcp-hub-worker during k157 (2026-11-07), by reading; docs only (POD in lib/MCP/Hub/Upstream/Http.pm).
(a) The req_ua POD says its request timeout "is all that bounds a request, over both transports". Over HTTP+SSE the request's own timer (request_timeout, _sse_request_p) bounds the answer on the stream; req_ua bounds only the POST that carries it. Pre-dates k157.
(b) The HTTP+SSE POST paragraph lists "no response" among the failures that fail "at once, with that reason". Since k157 a POST held past request_timeout (req_ua's "Request timeout") fails as "timed out after Ns" and is cancelled -- the paragraph can be read as contradicting that. The code comment above _sse_post already says so; the POD does not.
Done when: both POD passages say what the code does (SSE: request timer bounds the request, req_ua the POST; a timed-out POST of a request is the request timing out), nothing else changed.

Also check the comment above req_ua (Http.pm ~line 31, 'That timeout is all that bounds a request'): true for Streamable HTTP, not for an HTTP+SSE request, whose own timer bounds it.

